  "why": "The statement asks for the exact inequality χ ≤ h for every graph. Linear and polylog-type bounds (Kostochka–Thomason, Norin–Postle–Song, Postle, Delcourt–Postle, Liu–Luo, Norin–Steiner, Wood) leave the exact question open and are scored bound. Small-k cases and restricted graph classes are case. The dominating, odd, Seymour–Woodall, covering and spherical-valuation items are variants or different problems. The OpenAI family-157 manuscript claims random α ≤ 2 graphs with h < 26m/75 + 2/3 < m/2 ≤ χ. That contradicts the universal statement, so it is a whole-scope refutation with a non-explicit counterexample. Its only check is a Lean build that has not been independently inspected (F7), so it is unreviewed. It stays credible, because the only reason for doubt is that it is 8 days old and unreviewed (F10). The Postle withdrawal is a merger into Delcourt–Postle and not a retraction, so that event stays credible. Norin–Steiner is generated, because the authors say the model found the proof and almost none of their own ideas were kept. Wood is generated because Claude did the optimisation and worked out the details, which is the paper's content. The Ishihara claim cannot be dated and is set aside as not credible.",
  "doubts": "The OpenAI counterexample's probabilistic core was not fully read by the researcher. The Lean statement 'not_hadwiger_conjecture' has not been checked against the standard definition of the minor. If the claim holds, the Norin–Steiner linear theorem is unaffected, but every confirmed-direction case result is consistent only with small k. Pre-1990 venues come only from Wikipedia. Treating the linear theorem as a bound rather than a variant was a close call. I chose bound because χ ≤ C·h bounds the same quantity the statement asks about.",
